Z||000003||ray b||08-25-2001||10:11 PM||||v-6 rad is bigger that the stock 4 cyl one<BR>or archie can sell you a bigger one for the v-8<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000004||Nashco||08-25-2001||11:18 PM||||I'm sure it'd be pretty easy to adapt an aluminum radiator into it.  You can get them made to custom lengths (19,22,25" X 19"), and most two core aluminum radiators are more efficient than three core copper-brass radiators.  Keep in mind that when you go to a four core copper-brass radiator, you have about half as much airflow do to the thickness of the radiator that air has to pass through.  To be honest, I'm amazed at how many people go with a four core copper-brass radiator instead of three core aluminum....aluminum is much better for cooling, especially aftermarket (Griffin) units, not to mention more durable.<P>As far as stock units, I think you would be better of with an aftermarket unit, because they're more durable due to one-piece construction (no plastic side tanks) and more versatile because you can weld brackets on them anywhere.  Good luck!<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>P.S.  If you don't agree with something I've said, don't reply to me, reply with a general opinion...thanks!||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tina||08-25-2001||11:29 PM||||I'am running a custom copper/brass4-core in my 383.<BR>Done at a local shop.<BR>(Took them 2 days, but need to bring old one in for measurements)<BR>But seriously considering the"COOL IT"<BR> 4-core aluminum ,not because I'am not happy with the performance of the old one.<BR>It's just, the first time I saw one up close,<BR>I was SOLD.<P>You might wanna look at some Corvette rad's,too.Was told a while ago it would fit.<BR>Don't quote me on that, though. Never tried it. <P>Tina<BR>BTW No I'am not telling you to call Archie.<BR>Even though he's a nice guy.<BR>I too, gave up a long time ago, to reach him via E-MAIL. <P><p>[This message has been edited by Tina (edited 08-25-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||gtcash||08-26-2001||12:27 AM||||Get the 4 core Archie sells, bolts in no problems...I too had difficulty reaching him...but from what I hear he gets 100's of e-mails each day...that sucks...I have a hard time with ten to twenty!<BR>You can order it from him on line...his order people will answer your request much faster than a question to Archie!!!<BR>I have a 350 V8, 1984 Coupe...||||reg||6
Z||000007||artherd||08-26-2001||01:29 AM||||mmrm, I need a good radiator for the northstar project, and it wouldn't hurt if it looked super-duper-neat along the way, got pics/info?<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tina:<BR><B><BR>But seriously considering the"COOL IT"<BR> 4-core aluminum ,not because I'am not happy with the performance of the old one.<BR>It's just, the first time I saw one up close,<BR>I was SOLD.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000008||Nashco||08-26-2001||02:51 AM||||<A HREF="http://griffinrad.com/" TARGET=_blank>Griffin Radiator</A><P>A lot of the V8 folks know which Corvette radiator can be used...I can't recall.  If you find out which corvette radiator can be used, then you can order than specific application from Griffin.  I highly recommend you read their "Why aluminum is better" info, and check out their selection.  They are pretty pricey, but their quality is FAR superior to that of any other brands I've seen.  Every radiator they make looks like it belongs in a race car.<P>Another great source, for those that shop for prices and selection, is on Ebay (go figure, huh   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">).  Just go to <A HREF="http://www.ebaymotors.com" TARGET=_blank>Ebay</A> and search for "aluminum radiator"...voila!<P>If anybody out there knows what Corvette year works, or has pics of their existing fiero aluminum radiator, I'd love to hear more   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  V8 owners aren't the only ones who could benefit from the aluminum radiator...I want to cut weight   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT<P>*apparently I can only make a link to a website that is shorter than one text line...that sucks, I wanted to link to ebay search RESULTS...that's alright, you all know how to use it <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">*<p>[This message has been edited by Nashco (edited 08-26-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Tina||08-26-2001||09:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B>mmrm, I need a good radiator for the northstar project, and it wouldn't hurt if it looked super-duper-neat along the way, got pics/info? </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I know what you mean.<BR>When I first saw a picture of it(some flyer in my summit order),all I could think of is<BR>...hmm,how exactly would I hang it on my livingroom wall and where... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>On a sidenote.......<BR>When I first met by BF, he had his toolbox in the livingroom. And of course couldn'd help myself to crack a few jokes about it. Now who's laughing? Him of course.<P>Anyways this is what I could find on short notice<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.becool.com/img/custom.jpg"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.becool.com/img/pro_stock_mod.gif"> <BR>Still not exactly like the one in the flyer.<BR>That one had 2 fans(push/pull)which where silver in color as well.(matrial unknown)<P>Tina <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000010||PJ||08-26-2001||10:44 AM||||Ron Davis has a GM style all aluminum 2" radiator that is relatively easy to install in a Fiero. You'll need to modify the top bracket a little and buy a couple of trick hoses to splice into the stock ones. Its similar to a Griffin except the side tanks are welded instead of stamped. His web site is rondavisradiators.com, not much on it though but photos.<P>PJ<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||artherd||08-26-2001||02:41 PM||||Hrm, the Cool It, Griffith, and Ron Davis all look awesome, so what fits? ;)<P>PJ: have you done this install?<P>Mmrm, Evans NPG coolant, Al <BR>radiator, sweet :)<P>Tina, I promise I will refrain from making jokes about your boyfriend's Box.   ...<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tina:<BR><B> I know what you mean.<BR>When I first saw a picture of it(some flyer in my summit order),all I could think of is<BR>...hmm,how exactly would I hang it on my livingroom wall and where... :D<P>On a sidenote.......<BR>When I first met by BF, he had his toolbox in the livingroom. And of course couldn'd help myself to crack a few jokes about it. Now who's laughing? Him of course.<P>Hehehehhe:)<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000012||PJ||08-27-2001||12:11 AM||||I installed a off-the-shelf GM 24x16 Ron Davis radiator in the race car back in '97. It is 2" wide and a tiny bit taller than the stock GM unit so it is not a simple drop-in, but not at all difficult for someone with basic fabricating skills. Price back then was around $210.00. The upper fitting on the passenger side is 1.75" so you'll need a 1.75" to 1.50" 90° hose to splice into the stock hose. The bottom fitting on the driver side is 1.50" but required a hose spliced in also. I can't remember the specifics but will look if you need the info. The difference in water temps over the stock GM unit is awesome.<P>PJ<BR> ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Archie||08-27-2001||05:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Lou Christopher:<BR><B> Please do not tell me to call archie, I did E mail- no response.<BR>       Thanks<BR>              Lou Christopher</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That damn Archie, never around when you need him.||||reg||1
Z||000014||artherd||08-27-2001||05:47 PM||||Hi PJ, size should be all I need, I can fab Al (friends with TIG welders rule :)  If you have any more info re size/part #, etc. I'd appricate that.<P>Think I'm sold on the Ron Davis, I'll post a full writeup once it's in the car :)<P>Gotta love this:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.pro-touring.com/rondavisradiators/6_inch_large_small1.jpg">  Form following function.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by PJ:<BR><B>I installed a off-the-shelf GM 24x16 Ron Davis radiator in the race car back in '97. It is 2" wide and a tiny bit taller than the stock GM unit so it is not a simple drop-in, but not at all difficult for someone with basic fabricating skills. Price back then was around $210.00. The upper fitting on the passenger side is 1.75" so you'll need a 1.75" to 1.50" 90° hose to splice into the stock hose. The bottom fitting on the driver side is 1.50" but required a hose spliced in also. I can't remember the specifics but will look if you need the info. The difference in water temps over the stock GM unit is awesome.<P>PJ<BR> </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000015||Lou Christopher||08-27-2001||10:27 PM||||I want to thank everyone who responed for their help.  I am resuching a number of the areas suggested.  I will post the results of my survey when it is completed. I expect to make a decision within two or three days.<P>                       Lou Christopher<P><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||PJ||08-29-2001||11:16 PM||||Ben,<P>I checked my notes on the Ron Davis install, here are the details. The radiator p/n is RAD24163, 24" x 16". It is a single pass so the fittings and cap are in the same location as a stock unit. The inlet and outlet are larger than stock (1.5" and 1.75"). I used a 1.5" to 1.25" 90° reducer hose on the top left fitting spliced into the stock 1.25" hose. On the bottom right I used a 1.75" 90° hose to a 1.75" to 1.25" straight reducer hose connected right to the 1.25" hard tube. Hoses were Samco blue silicone from Pegasus Racing Supply, around $25 ea. You won't need to weld on the radiator. Its the top and bottom brackets that hold the radiator in place that will need a little tweaking.<P>PJ<BR> ||||reg||1
